---
<Base title="About"><div class="wrap"><header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow accent">Background & interests</p><h1>About me</h1></header><section class="about-grid page-intro"><div class="prose"><p>I’m Ethan Meitz, a mechanical engineering PhD researcher at Carnegie Mellon University and a DOE Computational Science Graduate Fellow. I work with Alan McGaughey and Gerald Wang, and expect to finish my PhD in Spring 2027.</p><p>Computation is the thread running through my work. I enjoy moving between the physics of materials, the details of a programming language, the realities of a supercomputer, and the questions that motivate a biological model.</p><p>I’m looking for AI-for-science research opportunities with ambitious scientific questions and the resources to pursue them. I care about developing tools because of the science they make possible.</p><p>I grew up in St. Paul, Minnesota, and studied mechanical engineering at Washington University in St. Louis before coming to Carnegie Mellon. Away from research, I draw, play drums, and build things.</p><div class="actions"><a class="text-link" href={socials.linkedin}>LinkedIn ↗</a><a class="text-link" href={socials.github}>GitHub ↗</a></div></div><figure class="about-photo"><Asset name="ethan-waterfront.jpeg" alt="Ethan Meitz" eager /></figure></section>
  <section class="personal-teaser section" style="border-top:1px solid var(--line)"><Asset name="americorps-upscaled.png" alt="The Habitat for Humanity AmeriCorps team together after a summer of building homes" /><div><p class="eyebrow accent">Service & community</p><h2>AmeriCorps & Habitat for Humanity</h2><p>In summer 2019, I served with AmeriCorps at Habitat for Humanity in St. Paul. As a construction volunteer facilitator, I led crews of 8–20 volunteers and homebuyers, taught safe tool use, and helped build affordable housing.</p><p>Teaching someone to use a tool for the first time was as meaningful as the construction itself. The experience shaped how I think about leadership, communication, and making unfamiliar work approachable.</p><div class="actions"><a class="text-link" href="https://www.habitat.org/">Habitat for Humanity ↗</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="section about-grid"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow accent">Art, rhythm & making things</p><h2>Art & drumming</h2><p>Drawing and painting give me another way to pay attention to the world. I especially enjoy animals, color, and the process of figuring out how to put what I see onto a page.</p><p>At WashU, I played with CRASH, a service-oriented improvisational drumming group using buckets and metal objects. We performed on campus and in the community, and shared drumming with kids through after-school programs and other community events.</p><p>I also like tinkering with electronics and building physical things. Some of those experiments live alongside my older engineering projects in the archive.</p><div class="actions"><a href={url('art/')}>See my artwork ↗</a><a href={url('fun/')}>Visit the playground ↗</a></div></div><Asset name="CRASH1.PNG" alt="CRASH, Ethan’s college improvisational percussion group, performing outdoors" /></section>
  <section class="narrow"><h2>Education & experience</h2><dl class="timeline"><div><dt>2023–2027</dt><dd>DOE Computational Science Graduate Fellowship</dd></div><div><dt>2022–2027</dt><dd>Mechanical Engineering PhD, Carnegie Mellon University<br /><span class="muted">Expected completion Spring 2027</span></dd></div><div><dt>2024–2025</dt><dd>Visiting Graduate Student, Argonne National Laboratory<br /><span class="muted">Advised by Arvind Ramanathan; project ongoing</span></dd></div><div><dt>2021–2022</dt><dd>Summer Associate, Institute for Defense Analyses<br /><span class="muted">Computer vision and scientific data analysis</span></dd></div><div><dt>Through 2022</dt><dd>BS and MS in Mechanical Engineering, Washington University in St. Louis</dd></div><div><dt>Summer 2019</dt><dd>AmeriCorps Construction Volunteer Facilitator, Habitat for Humanity</dd></div></dl></section>
</div></Base>
